## ABSTRACT
Recently, inorganic perovskite shows great potential in photocatalysis, while intrinsic instability against moisture seriously hinders its application in water splitting. Herein, a facile co-precipitation-calcination strategy is applied to prepare novel aqueous-stable CsPbBr3@MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N}$ -doped-C heterostructures ( $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-C})$ with excellent photoelectrochemical water oxidation performance. Unique N-Br halogen bonds between PyrrodicN from N-C frameworks and Br-atoms from CPB chemically immobilizes CPB on N-C layers, enhancing the aqueous-stability of CPB $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-C}$ by prohibiting CPB decomposition. Furthermore, benefitting from the typeII heterojunction at CPB and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ interface, system carrier-separation performance can be significantly improved. Meanwhile, acting as water oxidation center, $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ can obviously enhance the interfacial carrierinjection efficiency of CPB. The rationally designed CPB $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-C}$ achieves boosted photocurrent-density and carrier migration of $2.50\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ and $41.40\%$ , which are 9.61 and 12.24 times larger than pristine CPB. This work sets up a new strategy to synthesize CPB-based material with excellent aqueous-stability, for promising catalysis and other optoelectronic devices.
Keywords: aqueously stable; $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ ; MOFs; $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ ; photoelectrochemical water oxidation
## 1. Introduction
In the past decade, organic-inorganic perovskite has attracted increased attention as a promising candidate in photovoltaic devices.[1] Recently, inorganic perovskite materials (such as $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ , CPB) have also been demonstrated as potential alternatives for photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production or $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction,[2, 3] due to their wide range optical response capability, long carrier migration distance and low exciton binding energy.[4-6] By virtue of proper surface modification, inorganic perovskite can exhibit outstanding photocatalytic capability for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. For example, Huang et al. reported an efficient photocatalytic hydrogen production processes based on reduced-graphene caped CPB.[7] Xu et al. also successfully constructed $\mathrm{\bfC}_{3}\mathrm{\bfN}_{4}$ skeleton supported CPB as the photocatalytic materials for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.[8] However, the decomposition of CPB always happens in moisture environment which results in a quick dissociation process in aqueous conditions,[2, 9, 10] leading to the inactivation of catalysts and an unsatisfied long-circulation performance. Therefore, developing aqueous stable CPB-based material should be the key point to develop CPB photoanodes for high performance PEC water splitting.
Among the present strategies to enhance the moisture stability of CPB, such as surface coating,[11] element doping and surface passivation,[12-14] though the decomposition behavior of CPB can be suppressed, the PEC water oxidation kinetics are always inevitably hindered. For example, after coating CPB with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ,[15] humidity stability of the composites can be improved but the system water splitting kinetics becomes sluggish as pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ has been demonstrated to be a poor water splitting catalyst with unsatisfactory charge injection property.[16- 18] Therefore, it should be a great challenge to find out an efficient stabilizer for CPB, which not only enhances the aqueous stability but also maintains superior carrier transfer performance and water oxidation kinetics. In this regard, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) derived functional materials, such as ZIF-67 derived $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , MIL-88 derived $\mathrm{FeO_{x}}$ , have been widely confirmed as efficient PEC water oxidation photoanode materials for their abundant porous structures as well as promising catalytic activity.[19-23] For this reason, it should be of great importance and challenge to rationally design MOFs-derived functional materials to stabilize CPB in the purpose of achieving excellent aqueous stability and outstanding PEC water oxidation kinetics simultaneously.
Herein, CsPbBr3/metal-organic-frameworks derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N}$ -doped C core/shell composite $(\mathbf{CPB}@\mathbf{C0}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}/\mathbf{N}.$ C) with excellent aqueous stability and PEC water oxidation performance is demonstrated for the first time. It is found that unique N-Br halogen bonds can be formed between the Pyrrodic-N from N-C frameworks and the Br atoms from CPB nanocrystal. Due to this distinct chemical bonding immobilization effect introduced by the MOFsderived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ frameworks, the decomposition reaction of CPB caused by intrinsic moisture sensitivity can be effectively prohibited, therefore results in a greatly enhanced aqueous stability of $\mathrm{CPB@Co_{3}O_{4}/N.C}$ system. Meanwhile, it is found that by carefully tailoring the microstructure, the optical response and carrier migration performance of obtained CPB $\textcircled{\omega}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-C}$ can be efficiently enhanced. Moreover, by coupling with MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N-C}$ as water oxidation co-catalyst, the surface carrier injection efficient of pristine CPB can be greatly enhanced, leading to an obviously improved water oxidation kinetics. Benefiting from the synergistic effects, rationally designed $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}$ photoanode achieves greatly enhanced photocurrent density and carrier migration performance $(\eta_{\mathrm{separation}}\times\eta_{\mathrm{injection}})$ of $2.50\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ and $41.40\%$ compared with pristine CPB. This work sets up new strategy to synthesize CPB-based material with excellent aqueous stability, which proves to be promising for PEC water oxidation and potential to be applied to other optoelectronic devices.
## 2. Results and discussion
The $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}$ is prepared through a facile co-precipitation-calcination method, and Figure 1a illustrates the overall synthesis processes. Firstly, CsBr and $\mathrm{Pb}{\bf B}\mathrm{r}_{2}$ were used as the raw materials to prepare CsPbBr3 nanocrystals by a general low temperature injection method. As prepared CsPbBr3 nanocrystals were then dispersed in methyl acetate (solution A). Thereafter, cobalt acetylacetonate and 2-methylimidazole were successively added to the solution A and stirred rapidly. Later, due to the halogen bonding effect between the $\mathrm{Br^{-}}$ atoms from $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ and pyrrodic- $\cdot\mathrm{N}^{+}$ atom from 2-methylimidazole (the N-Br halogen bond formed between 2-MIM and CPB can be confirmed by XPS and the detailed reaction process are illustrated in Figure S1), and the crystallization effect of ZIF-67, $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ would coprecipitate with ZIF-67 and promote ZIF-67 uniformity coated on the surface of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ nanocrystals. Then, the precursor CPB/ZIF-67 was obtained after centrifugal drying. Thereafter, annealing at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air, the cobalt element in ZIF-67 was oxidized to $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . The N-doped Carbon framework is formed as the Natom containing ligand in ZIF-67, 2-methylimidazole, is pyrolyzed. The $\mathbf{N}$ atoms would spontaneously enter the carbon skeleton, and thereby realizing the in-situ N atom doping. After cooling, the sample $\mathrm{CPB}/\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}$ -doped C was obtained.
In Figure 1b-f, scanning electron microscopy (SEM), transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images and Xray diffraction patterns are depicted, which illustrate the morphology and phase component of obtained CPB $@$ ZIF67 and CPB $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-C}$ . From Figure 1b-c, it can be seen that due to the co-precipitation effect between ZIF-67 and CPB, ZIF-67 shell is uniformly coated on CPB core. From Figure 1f (line 3), the XRD peaks of both CPB and ZIF-67 can be clearly identified. Under further annealing in air, the previous ZIF-67 shell turns into discontinuous particle structure. In corresponding XRD patterns of $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}$ the diffraction peaks of (100), (110), (111), (210), (220) and (222) are in good agreement with that of cubic CPB (JCPDS 54-0752), and diffraction peaks of (220), (311), (222) and (400) peaks can be indexed to that of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (JCPDS 43-1003). For the CPB $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-C-}$ $x$ samples with different contents of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ , the XRD patterns are shown in Figure S2b.The peak intensity of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ increases with the precursor ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ amount increasing, confirming the increased relative amount of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ in CPB $\textcircled{a}{\bf C}{\bf o}_{3}{\bf O}_{4}/{\bf N}{\bf-C}{\bf-}x.$
Figure $1\mathrm{g}$ shows that CPB $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-C}$ is composed of CPB as core, with MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ as shell. The ZIF-67 derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ shell is composed of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles with obvious channels, which is beneficial to electrolyte infiltration and electrode/electrolyte interfacial reaction. From the high-resolution TEM image in Figure 1h, the interface between CPB and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ can be clearly identified. The marked lattice spacing of $2.38\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ and $2.06\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ can be indexed to (211) and (200) plane of CPB, and the lattice spacing of $2.43\mathrm{~\AA~}$ can be indexed to (311) plane of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . Most importantly, it must be pointed out that both the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and CPB nanocrystals are uniformly distributed in the amorphous N-doped C, which is not only beneficial to the carriers’ transportation, but also contributes to the enhanced aqueous stabilized performance of CPB nanocrystal. The detailed stabilization mechanism is discussed later. Within the select area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern shown in Figure 1i, the diffraction rings can be indexed to (211), (220) and (400) planes of CPB and (220), (531) and (444) planes of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . Herein, for comparison, the TEM image of pristine MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ is also collected and shown in Figure S4. It can be found that the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles are uniformly distributed on the amorphous N-doped C layers with a marked d-spacing of $2.43\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ indexed to (311) plane of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (Figure S4). And the SAED pattern shown in Figure S4c can be indexed to (111), (311), (220), (511), (400) and (440) planes of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ .
The chemical bonding state and composition components of the as-synthesized CP $B@C_{0_{3}}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.C$ are tested via X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). Figure S6a shows the survey spectrum, showing the presence of Cs, Pb, Br, Co, O, N and C. From Figure S6b-d, the detailed XPS spectra of element Cs ((3d3/2: 737.1 eV and 3d5/2: $724.8\mathrm{eV}$ ), Pb (4d5/2: 143.5 eV and 4d7/2: 138.7 eV) and Br $\mathrm{(2p1/2\colon188.6eV}$ and $2\mathrm{p}3/2\colon181.9\mathrm{eV}\rangle$ are shown. According to the work reported before, the Pb-O bonding ( $144.4\mathrm{eV}$ and $139.5\mathrm{eV}.$ ) shown in Figure S6c should be attributed to the surface oxygen-containing functional groups on CPB.[24-28] From Figure S6e-h, the detailed XPS spectra of element Co (3s orbital), O (1s orbital), N (1s orbital) and C (1s orbital) are shown. The existence of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ can be confirmed by obvious Co-O bonding deconvoluted from Co 3s ( $103.1\mathrm{eV}$ and $102.2\mathrm{eV}.$ and O1s ( $(529.3\mathrm{eV},$ $529.8\ \mathrm{eV},$ 531.3 eV) spectra.[29-33] Meanwhile, from the N 1s spectra, the existence of pyridinic N $(398.6\ \mathrm{eV})$ , pyrrodic N $(400.2\mathrm{eV})$ and graphitic N $\mathsf{J}(401.8\mathsf{e V})$ can be evidenced,[34] which is considered as the typical evidence to confirm N is doped in the MOFs-derived C layers. And from the C 1s spectra, the existence of C-N ( $285.4\mathrm{eV})$ , C $\mathrm{\Sigma^{\prime}-C(286.4\mathrm{eV})}$ and C-O $288.6\mathrm{eV})$ bonding can be clearly evidenced.[35-38] The existence of C-N bonding further confirms the formation of N-doped C layers.[35] It should be pointed out that, an addition pair of -Br¯ peaks and - $\mathbf{N}^{+}$ peak can be deconvoluted from the detailed XPS spectra of Br 3p and N 1s spectra,[8, 39] respectively. According to the work reported before, this phenomenon should be attributed to the formation of N-Br chemical halogen bonding between the pyrrodic-N from N-doped C framework and Br atoms form CPB nanocrystals. During this chemical reaction, $\mathbf{-N^{+}}$ and -Br¯ would act as Lewis acid and Lewis base, respectively.[39] After reaction, the Ndoped C layer would become rich with electron, while the CPB would become electron deficient. This conclusion can be well supported by the positively shifted binding energy of $\mathtt{B r3p}$ orbital comparing with pristine CPB (Figure 2), and the negatively shifted N 1s orbital comparing with pristine MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ , which confirms the N-doped C frameworks are electron rich, while the CPB is electron deficient.[8, 40] This unique N-Br halogen bonding at the interfaces between N-doped C framework and CPB would lead to a relatively stable chemical environment around $\mathrm{\CPB}@\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N-C}.$ and effectively prohibit the decomposition reaction of CPB, which greatly enhances the aqueous stability of C $\mathrm{PB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}$ system.
The optical response capability of as synthesized samples is characterized through UV-vis absorption spectra (Figure 3a). It can be seen that MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ can exhibits long range absorption ability from ultraviolet down to visible light region, but the optical capture efficiency is quite poor. As for pristine CPB, a unique absorption edge is observed at the wavelength range of $550~\mathrm{nm}$ Further combination with MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ , the system optical response range can be broadened to around $640~\mathrm{nm}$ . The integrated light absorption value within $300{-}650\mathrm{nm}$ of pristine CPB, C $\mathrm{{\cdotPB}}@\mathrm{{Co}}_{3}\mathrm{{O}}_{4}/\mathrm{{N}}\mathrm{{-C}}\mathrm{{-}}x\left(x{=}1,2,3,4\right)$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ is $42.6\%$ , $53.8\%$ , $56.2\%$ , $58.5\%$ , $59.1\ \%$ and $23.6~\%$ , respectively. Assuming the system can achieve $100\%$ internal quantum efficiency, the maximum electron flux achieved is estimated by integrating the absorbance across the $\mathbf{AM}1.5\mathbf{G}$ solar spectrum (Figure 3b).[41] After integrating the electron flux across the wavelength range of 300-650, the ideal photocurrent density $(\mathbf{J}_{a b s})$ should be 7.16, 9.04, 9.45, 9.84, 9.96 and $3.98\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ . Hence, it can be concluded that after coupling CPB with MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ , the system optical-response performance is greatly enhanced, leading to obviously enhanced charge carrier density. Similar conclusion can also be drawn from the light harvesting efficiency of as synthesized samples as shown in Figure S9.
The photo-induced carriers’ transfer and separation performance of as obtained samples are studied with steadystate photoluminescence (PL) spectrum and time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) spectrum. From Figure 3cd, it can be seen that both pristine CPB and MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ exhibit extremely high photoluminescence intensity, indicating the poor carrier separation performance. As for the $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}$ composites, the decreased PL intensity in comparison with pristine samples indicates that the photo-induced carriers’ separation performance is obviously enhanced. It can be seen that the CPB $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}3$ sample achieves the lowest PL intensity, indicating the best carrier separation and transportation performance. However, the carrier’s separation performance would be hindered with $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ amount further increasing, which might act as the carrier recombination centers. In Figure 3d, the time-resolved photoluminescence spectra are recorded to quantitatively measure the lifetime of photo-induced carriers.[42] The average carrier lifetime (τ) can be calculated by:
$$
\tau{=}\frac{B_{1}\tau_{1}^{2}+B_{2}\tau_{2}^{2}+B_{3}\tau_{3}^{2}}{B_{1}\tau_{1}+B_{2}\tau_{2}+B_{3}\tau_{3}}
$$
The measured time constants of the products are listed in Table S1. The pristine CPB and MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}}$ C exhibit carrier lifetime of 10.15 and $6.19\mathrm{ns}$ . After constructing $\mathrm{\DeltaTB@030_{4}/N.C}$ composites, the average lifetime is obviously enhanced. Especially, the CP $\left.\mathbf{B}@\mathbf{C}_{03}\mathbf{O}_{4}/\mathbf{N}_{-}\mathbf{C}_{-}\right.$ 3 sample achieves the longest carrier lifetime of 18.03 ns, which indicates that photogenerated carriers possess a longer lifetime and higher opportunity to be transferred and collected at the interface, confirming the unique $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}$ structure can efficiently enhance the photoinduced carrier’s separation performance.
Photovoltage decay measurement has been considered as an efficient way to characterize the presence of trap states in photoanode materials.[43] Herein, the photovoltage-time $(\mathrm{V}_{-}t)$ spectra of $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}._{\lambda}$ $(x{=}1,2,3$ , 4) and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ electrodes are collected under 3-electrode condition (Figure S11). As pristine CPB photoanode will be quickly dissolved in the electrolyte, the $\mathbf{V}{-}t$ plots of pristine CPB photoanode are inaccurate and not compared with other samples. The tested $\mathbf{V}_{-t}$ plots are fitted by biexponential function with two-time constants:[43]
$$
\begin{array}{c}{\displaystyle{\mathbf{y}(t)=A_{0}+A_{1}e^{-{\boldsymbol{\imath}}_{{\tau}_{1}}}+A_{2}e^{-{\boldsymbol{\imath}}_{{\tau}_{2}}}}}\\ {\displaystyle{\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{m}}=\frac{\boldsymbol{\tau}_{1}\times\boldsymbol{\tau}_{2}}{\boldsymbol{\tau}_{1}+\boldsymbol{\tau}_{2}}}}\end{array}
$$
Among which $\tau_{\mathrm{m}}$ is the harmonic mean of the lifetime and the total half lifetime can be calculated by:
$$
\tau_{h}=\log(2\times\tau_{m})
$$
The calculated total half lifetime of $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}-x\ (x{=}1,2,3,4)$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ electrodes are listed in Table S2. It can be seen that CPB $\textcircled{a}\mathbf{C}_{03}\mathbf{O}_{4}/\mathbf{N}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}3$ shows the fastest decay profile, indicating after rationally adjusting the component of $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathbf{C}.$ , the pristine surface trap state of CPB and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ can be efficiently passivated and the carrier loss caused by $\mathrm{{e^{-}/h^{+}}}$ recombining can be obviously reduced.
Based on the discussion before, to clarify the detailed photo-induced carrier’s transportation behavior among the CPB $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-C}$ system, the relative energy band position of the system is illustrated. Through ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) and TAUC function, both the band structure of pristine CPB and MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ can be calculated and shown in Figure S12.[44, 45] The conduction band (CB) and valence band (VB) position of pristine CPB are $-3.2\mathrm{eV}$ and $-5.5\mathrm{eV}.$ respectively. Both band positions are higher than that of MOFsderived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ (CB: $-2.7\mathrm{eV},$ VB: $-4.6\mathrm{eV}$ ). It can be concluded that the incorporation of CPB in MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ can lead to a uniquely matched type-II heterojunction (Figure S13). Under excitation, the photo-induced electrons from MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ would immediately be injected into the conduction band position of CPB. Meanwhile, the photo-induced holes from CPB will spontaneously injected into the valence band of MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ , which will then be consumed by the rapidly water oxidation reaction happens at the photoanode/electrolyte interfaces and lead to a further enhanced carrier separation performance.
The photoelectrochemical performance of pristine CPB, $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}.$ $x$ ( $\scriptstyle\chi=1$ , 2, 3, 4) and pristine MOFsderived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ are recorded and analyzed as follows. In Figure 4a, the linear sweep voltammograms (LSV) curves are shown. It can be seen that pristine CPB can exhibit certain photoelectrochemical activity, but the photocurrent density is quite poor $(0.25\mathrm{mAcm^{-2}}$ ). As for pristine MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ , within the low-bias range, the photocurrent density is quite poor. The low PEC performance of pristine CPB and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ should be attributed to their poor charge carrier separation capability.[46] However, the PEC performance of MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ in high-bias potential turns to be intense, attributing to its good catalyst performance. Further incorporating CPB in MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ , the system PEC performance is greatly enhanced comparing with sole material. The modest $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}3$ achieves the largest photocurrent density of $2.50\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ . In addition, it should be pointed out that, comparing with pristine CPB ( $\mathrm{0.28V}$ vs. RHE), CPB $\textcircled{a}\mathbf{C}_{0_{3}}\mathbf{O}_{4}/\mathbf{N}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}3$ exhibits obviously negatively shifted onset potential of $0.09{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE. It confirms that by incorporating CPB in MOFsderived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ , the surface water oxidation performance of pristine CPB can be efficiently enhanced, demonstrating the good co-catalyst effect and facilitated carrier separation performance.[40, 47] Furthermore, the PEC performance of C $\mathrm{PB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{-}$ C-4 sample shows further decreased photocurrent density. Considering the PL and V-t results discussed above, this result should be attributed to the hindered charge carrier separation performance of $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}.$ 4.
According to the recorded LSV curves, the applied bias photon-to-current conversion efficiency (ABPE) is recorded and shown in Figure 4b. It can be seen that CPB $\textcircled{\omega}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}3$ sample achieves the highest ABPE efficiency of $1.52\%$ at $0.45\mathrm{V}$ (vs. RHE), exhibiting a greatly enhanced ABPE efficiency comparing with pristine CPB $(\sim0.01\%)$ and MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N–C}\left(\mathrm{-0.08\\%}\right)$ at the same bias potential. The enhanced PEC performance achieved by $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}3$ clearly demonstrates the synergistic enhancement achieved by modifying CPB with MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N-C.[40]}$ While, the $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.($ -4 sample exhibits a decreased ABPE efficiency, which should be assigned to the aggravated carrier recombination from excess MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N}$ -C.[48]
The optical-response performance of obtained samples is recorded through chronoamperometric I-t curves under chopped illumination.[21, 49] In Figure $\scriptstyle4\mathrm{c}$ , it can be seen that except for pristine CPB, all samples show rapid optical-response to the illumination On-Off circulation. With a bias potential of $1.23{\mathrm{V}}$ (vs. RHE), CPB $\mathsf{i}@\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}/\mathbf{N}.$ C-3 sample achieves the largest photocurrent density of $1.36\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , which is obviously larger than that of pristine CPB and MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ , in accordance with the results of LSV. It should be pointed out that pristine CPB exhibits certain optical response. However, due to the poor aqueous stability, the photocurrent density of CPB photoanode quickly decays to zero (about $50~\mathrm{s}$ ). Illuminating CPB photoanode again, the photocurrent density cannot increase again, which should be attributed to the thorough decomposition of pristine CPB. However, after incorporating CPB in MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N-C}$ the photocurrent density of $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}.x$ ( $\scriptstyle\chi=1$ , 2, 3, 4) turns to be relative stable. This indicates that the decomposition reactions of CPB can be effectively suppressed, resulting in an obviously enhanced aqueous stability of $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}$ .
To further evaluate the intrinsic PEC performance enhancement achieved by incorporating pristine CPB in MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ , the photoanodes are renormalized by the electrochemical active surface area (ECSA).[40] According to previously reported method, the ECSA data of pristine MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ and $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}.$ $x$ $(x{=}1,2,3,4)$ are collected in Figure S14 and listed in Table S3. It can be seen that the ECSA of pristine MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ and CPB $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}x$ $(x{=}1,2,3,4)$ ) is calculated to be 2.79, 2.89, 3.05, 3.24 and $2.63\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . According to the ECSA data, the photocurrent density of the obtained photoanodes is renormalized to be 0.15, 0.6, 0.66, 0.77 and $0.45\mathrm{mAcm}^{2}$ (at a bias potential of $1.23{\mathrm{V}}$ vs. RHE and shown in Figure S15). The photocurrent density enhancement trend achieved by $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}.3$ does not change comparing with the photocurrent density normalized by the geometrical area. This result confirms the intrinsic PEC enhancement achieved by the synergistic effect of coupling pristine CPB with MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ .
The incident photon-to-current conversion efficiency (IPCE) plots of obtained CPB, CPB $\textcircled{a}\mathbf{C}_{0_{3}}\mathbf{O}_{4}/\mathbf{N}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}3$ and MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ photoanodes are recorded and shown in Figure S16 to illustrate the concurrent enhancement. Pristine CPB photoanode can achieves a max IPCE intensity of $35\%$ and quickly decreases to 0 at $550~\mathrm{nm}$ , which is in accordance with the absorbance spectra profile of pristine CPB. In addition, MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ can exhibit PEC reactivity in the whole optical range but the IPCE intensity is quite low. This phenomenon comes from the low light-capture capability and intense carrier recombination property of MOFsderived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ . Incorporating pristine CPB in MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ , the IPCE intensity of the obtained CPB $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-C}$ is greatly enhanced. The enhanced IPCE intensity in the wavelength range of $300{-}550~\mathrm{{nm}}$ attributes to the enhanced carrier separation and surface water oxidation kinetics brought by the MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ . In addition, the IPCE intensity beyond the wavelength range of $550\mathrm{nm}$ also shows certain enhancement. It should be assigned to the optical-response caused by MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ . Hence, it can be concluded that by coupling pristine CPB with MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ , the system light harvesting capability, surface water oxidation kinetics and system carrier separation processes all can be greatly enhanced and therefore leads to obviously improved PEC performance.
In addition, the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) is also applied to investigate the relationship between the surface water oxidation, carrier transport and PEC performance of obtained samples. It should be pointed out that as the complete EIS testing would take a relative long time (about 150 s), hence the EIS plots of pristine CPB photoanode is hard to collect due to the poor aqueous stability of it. For other samples, the EIS plots are collected and shown in Figure 4d. The equivalent circuit model of as prepared photoanodes are also shown in Figure 4d. The Rs, Rct and C indicates to the series resistance, charge transfer resistance and space charge region capacitance, respectively. W refers to the Warburg impedance. In addition, the Rpt and Cpt refers to the resistance and capacitance at the Pt cathode.[50] As shown in Figure 4d, the EIS plots can be divided into two parts. According to the work reported before, the small semicircle at the high frequency refers to the charge transfer resistance (Rct) and the straight line at the low frequency region refers to the mass transfer resistance.[19, 50, 51] From Figure 4d, it can be seen that C $\mathrm{\DeltaPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}3$ achieves the smallest charge transfer resistance and mass transfer resistance.[19, 50, 51] This phenomenon demonstrates that by rationally combining pristine CPB with MOFsderived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ , the surface water oxidation kinetic and interfacial carrier transportation performance can be greatly enhanced.
To take further investigation on the influence of carrier migration enhancement on the PEC performance of obtained CPB, CP $B@C_{03}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.$ C-x $\scriptstyle\chi=1$ , 2, 3, 4) and MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ photoanodes, the system charge separation (ηseparation) and interfacial charge injection $(\eta_{\mathrm{injection}})$ efficiency are recorded.[40, 52] As shown in Figure S18a, the modest $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}3$ achieves the largest charge carrier separation efficiency of $67.1\ \%$ ( $\mathrm{1.23\:V}$ vs. RHE), which is greatly enhanced comparing with pristine CPB ( $18.89~\%$ and MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ $(23.13~\%)$ . It confirms that by combing pristine CPB with MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ , with the unique interfacial type-II heterojunction, the system carrier recombination can be efficiently suppressed and therefore leads to greatly enhanced PEC performance.[40] In Figure S18b, the interfacial carrier injection efficiency of as obtained samples is shown. $\mathrm{\DeltaTB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}3$ achieves the highest carrier injection efficiency of $61.7\%$ ( $1.23\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE), which is also higher than that of pristine CPB $(17.94\%$ and MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ $(39.12\ \%)$ . It directly confirms that MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N-C}$ acting as water oxidation co-catalyst can efficiently enhance the surface water oxidation kinetics of pristine CPB.[40, 47] However, too much MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ may decrease the system water oxidation kinetics, resulting from the extensive carrier recombination originated from the excessive loading of MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ . It can be concluded that as synthesized $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}3$ achieves a greatly enhanced carrier migration performance, which can be determined by $\eta_{\mathrm{separation}}\times\eta_{\mathrm{injection}}$ of $41.40\%$ .
To further investigate the aqueous stability of the rationally designed C $\mathrm{PB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}$ , long circular chronoamperometric I-t curves of as prepared photoanodes are recorded. From the inset of Figure 5a, it can be seen that pristine CPB exhibits quite poor aqueous stability. The photocurrent density of CPB decays to zero within 50 second. After coupling CPB with MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ , both photocurrent density and stability are greatly enhanced. The photocurrent density of CPB ${\mathrm{;}}\textcircled{a}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.$ C-3 can maintain $87\%$ initial value after circulating for 24 h. Meanwhile, the absorbance spectra of photoanode during circulation are recorded and shown in Figure 5b. It is revealed that the characteristic absorption edge of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ and CPB shows no obvious change. From the viewpoint of photoluminescence stability, the aqueous stability of pristine CPB, C $\mathrm{{}^{3}B@C o_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ is further investigated. As shown in Figure S19a, during the circulation reaction, the $\mathrm{PL}$ intensity of pristine CPB almost quenches to zero quite soon. However, the PL intensity of as designed CPB $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-C}$ is quite stable, which only decreases by ${\sim}15~\%$ in $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , which further confirms that the aqueous stability of pristine CPB can be efficiently enhanced by coupling CPB with MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ (Figure 5c). XRD patterns of $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}3$ photoanode before and after circulation 24 hours are collected. It can be seen that after circulation $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}3$ shows evident peaks of CPB and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (Figure 5d). Also, as shown in Figure S20, TEM image and SAED pattern of CPB $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-C}$ circulated $24\mathrm{h}$ are recorded. It can be seen that both the morphology and phase component of $\mathrm{CPB@Co_{3}O_{4}/N.C}$ are stable. This further confirms the aqueous stability achieved by CPB $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-C}$ .
As it is confirmed in XPS results, pyridinic N, pyrrodic $\mathbf{N}$ and graphitic N can be evidenced from the MOFsderived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ frameworks (Figure 6a). Uniquely, N-Br halogen bond can be formed between the pyrrodic N atoms and Br atoms from the CPB nanocrystal, which therefore can chemically immobilize the CPB nanocrystal on the N-C layers (Figure 6b). According to the work reported before, the pyrrodic N atoms from N-C and Br atoms from CPB can be regarded as Lewis acid and Lewis base,[8, 39] which would bond spontaneously and therefore are fixed CPB nanocrystal with N-C frameworks (Figure 6c-d). The unique interfacial bonding effect between $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}}$ C framework and CPB can effectively prohibit the decomposition reaction caused by intrinsic moisture sensitivity of CPB. Therefore, the aqueous stability of $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}$ can be greatly enhanced. $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C P B}$ is also studied to further confirm the chemical bonding immobilization influence on the aqueous stability of CPB $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-C}$ The component and structure of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C P B}$ are illustrated in Figure S21a-b. Within the sample of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C P B}$ , the interfacial chemical bonding immobilization effect can be excluded. The corresponding chronoamperometric I-t curves are shown in Figure 6a. It can be seen that, without the chemical bonding immobilization effect introduced by N-Br halogen bond, the aqueous stability of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C P B}$ is quite poor comparing with CP $B@C_{0_{3}}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.C$ . From the circulation-photoluminescence spectra shown in Figure S19b, it can also be evidenced that the PL intensity of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C P B}$ decays much faster comparing with as-designed $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}$ . Hence, it can be concluded that benefiting from the unique interfacial N-Br halogen bond, the aqueous stability of $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}$ can be efficiently enhanced.
Based on the discussion above, the possible schematic of PEC reaction and aqueous stability enhancement achieved by $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}$ is illustrated and shown in Figure 7. It can be seen that, firstly, due to the unique component characteristic of MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ frameworks, CPB nanocrystal can be fixed on the framework under the chemical bonding immobilization effect of N-Br halogen bond, which provides a relatively stable chemical environment for $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}$ and prohibits the decomposition of CPB, resulting in an improved long-cycling stability in aqueous condition. Secondly, benefitting from the unique type-II heterojunction formed at the interface of CPB and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ , the system carrier separation and transportation performance can be significantly improved. Thirdly, acting as water oxidation co-catalyst, MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ can rapidly consume the photoinduced holes from CPB and oxidize the OH- from electrolyte into $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , thus further promoting carrier separation capability. Therefore, by rationally designing and regulating the components of $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.$ C compounds, the carrier separation and interfacial water oxidation kinetics of pristine CPB can be obviously improved, while the aqueous stability of $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}$ system can be improved.
## 3. Conclusions
In summary, a brand-new $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{3}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.$ -doped C heterostructure is synthesized for the first time through a facile co-precipitation-calcination strategy. Coupled CPB with MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ framework, both the aqueous stability and PEC performance can be greatly enhanced. Evidently, by carefully adjusting system component, the $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}.3$ sample achieves the best PEC water oxidation performance of $2.50\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and carrier migration performance $(\eta_{\mathrm{separation}}\times\eta_{\mathrm{injection}})$ of $41.33\ \%$ . Circulated for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ in aqueous solution, $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}$ system shows no phase-component change, which renders CPB as potential photoanode material for PEC water oxidation reaction. This outstanding aqueous stability and PEC performance achieved by $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}$ should be attributed to the following effects. Firstly, due to the N-Br halogen bond formed at the interface of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ framework and CPB nanocrystal, CPB can be chemically immobilized to prohibit the decomposition of pristine CPB and therefore leads to greatly improved aqueous stability of $\mathrm{CPB@Co_{3}O_{4}/N.C}$ system. Secondly, the carrier separation performance of pristine CPB can be greatly enhanced benefiting from the unique type-II heterojunction at the interface of CPB and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ . Lastly, acting as co-catalyst, the surface water oxidation kinetics of pristine CPB can be fundamentally enhanced by the uniquely introduced MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N_{-}C}$ . This work opens a new method to design CPB-based high performance PEC water oxidation catalyst materials with outstanding aqueous stability and surface water oxidation performance. Comparing with other researches, this work provides a facile method to construct aqueous stable CPB-based material for photoelectrochemical cells, solar cells and photodetectors.
## 4. Experimental Section Preparation of $\mathbf{CPB}@\mathbf{Co_{3}O_{4}/N.C}$ Preparation of CPB nanocrystal
The CPB nanocrystal can be synthesized by a facile low temperature method. Firstly, 0.2 mmol CsBr and 0.2 mmol $\mathrm{Pb}{\mathbf{B}}\mathrm{r}_{2}$ are added into $5\mathrm{mlN}$ , N-dimethylformamide (DMF) mixture (containing $0.5~\mathrm{ml}$ oleic acid (OA) and $0.1{\mathrm{ml}}$ n-octylamine (OctAm)). The mixed solution was vigorously stirred under $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ to get a clarified solution. Subsequently, the precursor solution was quickly injected into $30\mathrm{ml}$ toluene (preheated to $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and kept stirred for 30 second. After the reaction process, the solution was further washed with methyl acetate and centrifuged at $3000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $5\mathrm{min}$ . The supernatant phase was discarded and the precipitated CPB nanocrystal was then further treated by the same method for twice. Finally, the precipitated CPB nanocrystal is re-dispersed into $50~\mathrm{ml}$ methyl acetate for further use.
## Preparation of CPB $@$ ZIF-67 precursor
As prepared CPB methyl acetate precursor solution was vigorously stirred to make sure the CPB nanocrystal was uniformly dispersed in methyl acetate. Then a certain amount of cobalt acetoacetate was added into the precursor solution $(\mathrm{W}_{\mathrm{Co}}{=}1,2,3,4\mathrm{mM})$ . After thoroughly mixing, 2-methylimidazole was quickly added $(\mathsf{W}_{\mathsf{C o}}\colon\mathsf{W}_{2}$ - $_\mathrm{MIM}{=}1:1.15)$ . Stirring for 1.5 hours, as obtained CPB $@$ ZIF-67 was centrifuged and washed with methyl acetate for three times and dried in vacuum for 24 hours. By adjusting the added cobalt acetoacetate and 2-MIM amounts, as obtained CPB $@$ ZIF-67 precursors are named as CPB $@$ ZIF-67- $x$ $\scriptstyle\chi=1$ , 2, 3, 4).
## Preparation of $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{C}_{93}\mathbf{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}$ -doped C
As prepared CPB $@$ ZIF-67 precursors were the annealed at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 4 hours with a temperature increasing rate of $1^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}$ can be obtained. And according to the naming principle of precursor CPB $@$ ZIF-67, the final products were named as $\mathrm{CPB}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}.$ $x$ $(x{=}1,2,3,4)$ , respectively.
To synthesis the sample $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C P B}$ , all procedures were similar as stated before, but substitute 2-methylimidazole with $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ 1 $(\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{Co}}:\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{OH}}{=}1:2)$ .
## Materials characterization
The X-ray diffraction (XRD) spectrum was taken in a 2θ range of $10–70^{\circ}$ . Scanning electron microscopy images were collected via SU-70 equipped with energy dispersive system. Transmission electron microscopy was carried out in Tecnai 20U-Twin equipment. The UV-vis diffusive reflectance spectra were tested by TU-1900. The PL spectra were tested with Hitachi U‐4100 with an excitation wavelength of $300\mathrm{nm}$ . The TRPL spectra were recorded with FLS920. The plots were measured with an excitation leaser of $400\mathrm{nm}$ and detected at $520\mathrm{nm}$ . In addition, the PL and UV-Vis spectra were tested with powder samples.
Photoanode preparation:
To construct the photoanode, as synthesized $50\mathrm{mg}$ power sample was dispersed in $200\upmu\mathrm{l}$ acetylacetone and then spin-coated onto the FTO substrate. Subsequently, the photoanode was calcinated at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ in air. [53] Photoelectrochemical performance characterization.
The PEC performance was test by a CHI660E electrochemical station. The applied electrolyte was consisted of $1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{NaOH}$ . Before test, the system was bubbled with Ar to remove the dissolved gas. In this text, the photoanode potential was converted into reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) potential according to Nernst equation:[19, 50]
$$
\mathrm{E_{RHE}{=}E_{A g/A g C l}{+}0.059p H{+}E_{\ A g/A g C l}^{\circ}}
$$
where ERHE was the converted potential versus RHE, $\mathrm{E_{Ag/AgCl}}$ was the measured potential versus the $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ electrode, and $\mathrm{E^{o}_{A g/A g C l}=0.1976\ V}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
